The closest earlier attempt was commit 336facf825 on 2026-10-14, titled "No save-or-discard prompts; ask only about files opened before". It is the only earlier attempt at this problem I found. It failed for one reason: it fixed the places on its list, and the header menus weren't on that list.

**What that attempt did.** On 2026-09-29 Kenneth decided JusPrin should never ask a person to save or discard; autosave keeps the work and version history is the way back. The 2026-10-14 commit went through the places that still asked or refused, one at a time:
- the AI's tool for opening a project;
- the AI's tool for setting up a printer;
- saving a filament;
- opening a project file;
- changing a printer's nozzle;
- connecting and renaming printers.

Each got its own fix and its own harness check, and the commit message lists each one.

**Why it missed the header menus.** The commit worked from a list of known places, not from the thing that actually draws the window. That thing is OrcaSlicer's `Tab::select_preset`: whenever the current preset has unsaved edits, it shows "Transfer or discard changes". Any JusPrin code that calls it can bring the window back. The header printer, filament and process menus all call it through `SetupCommands.cpp`. That file isn't among the files the commit changed, and its message doesn't mention the header menus. Searching for every JusPrin caller of `select_preset` would have turned these up; working through a list didn't.

There was also a sign in the code that the menu expected the window. The header printer menu was written two weeks before the sweep, in 1c010451cb, and its comment says "Orca kept the current printer, including when its prompt was cancelled". In other words, the menu was built assuming OrcaSlicer would ask. Nobody revisited it when the rule changed.

**Why the tests didn't catch it.** The harness checks added in 336facf825 covered exactly the places that were fixed. The header-menu checks that already existed switched printers with no unsaved edits, and the window only appears when there are unsaved edits. So every test passed while a person with edits would still see the window.

**Why Kenneth hit it without editing anything.** This part is partly my inference. The window listed values that look identical: the same junction value three times, and thumbnails that differ only by a space. OrcaSlicer's preset comparison counts those as edits. I confirmed the comparison flags them: the thumbnails rule that is meant to ignore spacing can never run (upstream OrcaSlicer has the same flaw), and his "Generic printer" profile has three extruder variants, hence three junction rows. I have not confirmed how those leftover differences got into his preset in the first place. So the window can appear for people who changed nothing, which makes the missed path more likely to be hit than the sweep's tests suggested.

**How today's fix avoids the same gap.** The new check, which saves to version history and then drops the edits, sits inside the `SetupCommands` functions that call `select_preset`, not in each menu. So every menu that uses them is covered, including ones added later. It still doesn't cover OrcaSlicer's own settings window, whose drop-downs call `select_preset` directly; I expect that one still asks, but haven't checked.

